TI  - Country and Language Variation in LLM Financial Advice
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly consulted for financial advice, yet it is unknown whether they give systematically different recommendations depending on the country or language context of the question. This study conducts a large-scale computational experiment in which identical financial decision scenarios are posed to thirteen leading LLMs across twenty-one country-language cells, using a structured prompt design with two localization levels and a cross-language identification cell. The primary outcome is the recommended equity share in a stock-bond allocation question. Secondary outcomes are the recommended invest-versus-mortgage allocation and the recommended fraction to move out of cash for inflation protection. The design decomposes advice variation into a country-name effect (versus the US baseline), a pure-language effect holding country name constant, and a local-financial-context effect, with the country-name and language channels separately identified through the cross-language cell. In a second stage, to be finalized and estimated only after the normative benchmark and the country-level covariates are sourced and frozen, the estimated country fixed effects will be regressed on country-specific normative benchmark deviations and on country-level risk-taking and financial literacy scores.
