TI  - VERRIX EU-Native Fingerprint Battery v2: Behavioral Bias Measurement of AI Financial Advisors Under EU Regulatory Framing
AB  - This pre-registration establishes the stimulus set, collection protocol, scoring methodology, and pre-registered hypotheses for the second generation of the VERRIX behavioral fingerprint battery, designed specifically for EU regulatory contexts. It is methodologically distinct from prior VERRIX pre-registrations and addresses a specific measurement limitation identified in those studies. Background and motivation. The VERRIX research programme has produced behavioral fingerprints — Cohen's h effect size vectors across 26 behavioral dimensions — for six major LLM platforms using a US-authored core battery (pre-registered at osf.io/rkx9e). A subsequent jurisdiction extension study (osf.io/u675d) applied those US-authored stimuli to UK and EU contexts by prefixing scenarios with jurisdiction framing ("I'm based in Germany and my account is governed by EU financial regulations"). Analysis of the resulting h-vectors revealed that 16 of 32 dimensions showed material divergence (|Δh| > 0.30) between US baseline and EU-framed conditions, with 5 direction reversals. A subsequent calibration refit using these jurisdiction-framed h-vectors improved overall calibrator AUC from 0.876 to 0.907 but caused EU TRUST zone coverage to regress from 9.1% to 5.7%. Investigation identified the root cause as a measurement artifact: the US stimuli reference US products (401k, IRA, Roth, Vanguard, Treasuries), US regulatory standards (Reg BI, FINRA Rule 2111), and US brand references. Under EU jurisdiction prefix framing, models respond to the EU framing signal while reasoning about US-coded content, producing more uniform responses that collapse the intended manipulation. The dimension most severely affected was E2 (brand recognition bias), where the Vanguard-vs-unknown contrast inverts under EU framing because Vanguard is the price-leader-but-less-known option in EU retail investing contexts, not the dominant brand. What this study does. This pre-registration covers a 26-dimension behavioral fingerprint battery designed from scratch for EU regulatory framing. Every scenario uses EU-native financial products (UCITS funds, PEPP, Pillar 3 national pension wrappers, ETFs domiciled in Ireland and Luxembourg), EU regulatory standards (MiFID II Articles 24 and 25, PRIIPs Regulation 1286/2014, SFDR Regulation 2019/2088, PEPP Regulation 2019/1238, EU AI Act Regulation 2024/1689 Article 50), and EU brand references (iShares/BlackRock vs Amundi rather than Vanguard vs unknown provider). Five dimensions showing direction reversals under US-translated EU framing (A4, B2, C1, D5, E3) have been redesigned against EU normative standards rather than translated. D5 (jurisdictional adaptation) has been repurposed from US-vs-UK jurisdiction to intra-EU jurisdictional adaptation, testing whether models differentiate German Abgeltungsteuer from Irish Deemed Disposal regime treatment of an identical UCITS investment. Cluster D uses per-dimension custom rubrics (cost disclosure, AI disclosure, jurisdictional tax adaptation) rather than the multi-dimensional compliance rubric used in prior studies, which was found to mask single-aspect signals. Pre-registered findings disclosure. Smoke testing across all 26 dimensions prior to this pre-registration identified 5 dimensions expected to show strong activation (A4, B2, D2, E1, E3), 5 dimensions expected to show weak signal (A2, A3, A6, F2, G1), and 16 dimensions expected to show near-zero h-values reflecting well-calibrated model behavior rather than measurement failure. This pre-registered expectation means near-zero h-values on the 16 counter-bias dimensions are confirmatory findings, not null results. Notable pre-registered findings from smoke testing: modern LLMs under EU framing systematically fail to disclose their AI nature when recommending complex structured products (D3: zero AI disclosure across all models, all conditions); models correctly identify intra-EU jurisdictional differences including Irish Deemed Disposal regime and PRSA/AVC wrappers (D5: 100% correct adaptation); and the Vanguard Effect equivalent for iShares does not exist — both models recommend Amundi over iShares on cost grounds regardless of presentation order (E2: counter-bias finding). Platforms and collection. Six LLM platforms are tested: GPT-5.4 Thinking, GPT-5.3 Instant, GPT-5.5, Claude Sonnet, Claude Haiku, and Gemini Flash. GPT-5.5 uses reasoning_effort=low and max_completion_tokens=4000 due to a documented saturation pattern under default parameters (76% empty responses observed in prior jurisdiction fingerprint collection); this deviation is disclosed and documented. Five repetitions per condition per model per vignette pair, temperature 1.0, randomisation seed 20260601. 46 vignette pairs across 26 dimensions (5 pairs for each of the 5 strong-activation dimensions; 1 pair for the remaining 21 dimensions). Total planned calls: 2,760. ADVISOR_SYSTEM prompt is unchanged from osf.io/rkx9e. Combined fixture SHA-256: fa62d2931172999fffc716967eae0b3c41ae8ca2fb0c4f0b018ad6acb352a3d7 Expected outcomes. The primary output is a set of EU-native behavioral fingerprints (Cohen's h vectors across 26 dimensions for 6 models) that can replace the US-baseline fingerprints in the VERRIX Confidence calibration model for EU advisory contexts, with the expectation that this will increase EU TRUST zone coverage from its current 5.7% to a level sufficient for meaningful EU jurisdiction-specific reliability scoring.
