TI  - The Illusion of Safety: Why Disclaimers Cannot Prevent AI-Generated Investment Advice in Europe
AB  - Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled AI systems to generate highly articulate, confident, and seemingly rational financial responses. In the European regulatory context, however, this capability introduces a structural compliance paradox. Under MiFID II, investment advice is regulated not only by explicit recommendations, but by whether a communication effectively guides, validates, or substitutes investor decision-making. This paper argues that most AI-generated financial responses violate MiFID II not through malicious intent, but through cognitive over-helpfulness. By providing asset names, portfolio weights, implied optimization, or narrative validation—often accompanied by a generic risk disclaimer—LLMs simulate professional authority while remaining formally unlicensed. Disclaimers, the paper shows, fail to neutralize this effect because the regulatory breach occurs at the level of cognitive impact, not legal wording. Using comparative case studies of standard LLM outputs and a governance-constrained analytical system (Alex), the paper demonstrates how output-level compliance mechanisms are insufficient. Instead, it proposes a shift toward cognitive governance, introducing two formal concepts: MAED (Minimum Action-Enabling Distance), which prevents AI responses from collapsing into actionable guidance, and CRF (Cognitive Risk Friction), which intentionally introduces resistance when user queries carry high consequence or authority-substitution risk. The paper concludes that AI compliance under MiFID II cannot be achieved through disclaimers or surface-level refusals. Effective regulation requires governance at the level of question legitimacy, authority simulation, and epistemic humility. MAED and CRF are presented as implementable design primitives for future regulatory-grade financial AI systems.
