TI  - Generative AI and Financial Planning & Analysis: Empirical Evidence on Productivity Gains, Forecast Accuracy, and Implementation Success Factors
AB  - Financial Planning and Analysis (FP&A) has long operated under structural constraints that limit its ability to reconcilespeed, accuracy, and analytical depth. Despite incremental advances in statistical modeling and enterprise systems,forecasting processes remain largely manual, resource intensive, and poorly equipped to respond to increasinglyvolatile business environments. The recent diffusion of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI), particularly LargeLanguage Models (LLMs), introduces a qualitatively different set of capabilities with the potential to alter this equilibrium.This study presents a systematic synthesis of empirical evidence published between 2020 and 2025 on the applicationof GenAI in FP&A complemented by a proof-of-concept implementation conducted in a real organizational setting.Drawing on peer-reviewed research and reports from leading consulting institutions, the analysis documents productivitygains ranging from 30% to 50% and forecast accuracy improvements between 20% and 50% under favorableimplementation conditions. At the same time, the findings reveal a pronounced return-on investment asymmetry: while median financial returns from AI initiatives in finance remain close to 10%, a subset of organizations consistently achieves returns exceeding 20%. To explain this divergence, the study applies an Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP) perspective to identify and rank critical success factors associated with GenAI adoption in FP&A. Technology infrastructure emerges as the mostinfluential determinant, followed by process redesign, organizational change management, and environmentalconstraints. The proof-of-concept implementation corroborates these results, demonstrating a 45% reduction in forecastingcycle time and a 38% improvement in forecast accuracy, while also exposing persistent barriers related to data quality,governance, and human adoption. The paper concludes by outlining a pragmatic roadmap for responsible and valueoriented GenAI adoption in FP&A and by identifying priority areas for future empirical research.
