TI  - Impact of Artificial Intelligence on Investment Decision-Making among Retail Investors
AB  - Retail investors increasingly use artificial intelligence (AI) tools such as robo-advisors, screeners and chatbots, but it is unclear whether this improves decisions or amplifies bias. This study aims to evaluate the impact of AI-enabled investment tools and behavioral biases on the investment decision quality of Indian retail investors, with financial literacy examined as a moderating factor. A quantitative survey design was used. Data came from a structured questionnaire of twenty five-point Likert statements completed by 255 retail investors who use AI-driven platforms, and were analysed with descriptive statistics, Cronbach’s alpha, Pearson correlation and t-tests in SPSS. Respondents reported moderately high AI usage (mean 3.603), high financial literacy (3.835) and moderate behavioral bias (3.247). AI usage correlated positively with investment outcomes (r = 0.168, p = 0.007) and with bias scores (r = 0.211, p < 0.001), but not with financial literacy (r = 0.085, p = 0.176). Overconfidence, information overload, herding and the disposition effect were significant among AI users; overtrading was not (p = 0.068). The findings suggest that AI is a modest decision-support tool that coexists with persistent behavioral bias rather than removing it. For practice, platform developers should add explainability and bias-mitigating prompts, and regulators should prioritize disclosure and investor education. Future research should use longitudinal designs and linked brokerage records to test causality. In conclusion, AI tools help retail investors only modestly, so they work best when combined with financial knowledge and disciplined judgement.
