TI  - Robot Morality Study 1
AB  - This study investigates how consumers’ chronic motivational orientation shapes their moral evaluation of AI chatbots when the bots commit violations of fundamental moral principles in the context of recommending and purchasing a travel package. Drawing on Moral Foundations Theory and Self-Determination Theory, we distinguish between two types of violations—harm violations and authority violations—and examine how individuals’ trait-level motivation (effectance orientation vs. affiliation orientation) influences moral condemnation. We test an interaction effect in which individuals high in effectance orientation show heightened condemnation of harm violations, while individuals high in affiliation orientation show stronger condemnation of authority violations. Participants will engage in real-time interactions with an AI chatbot simulating an investment recommendation scenario. The study aims to demonstrate that moral judgments of AI are contingent not only on the type of violation but also on users’ underlying motivational traits. Expected contributions include advancing theory by linking moral cognition with relational need perspectives and offering practical guidelines for designing AI systems that account for user heterogeneity in motivational orientation.
