TI  - Fail-Closed AI: Why Architectural Guarantees Are Required for Regulated Deployment
AB  - Large language models cannot be deployed in regulated domains because no existing approach can guarantee policy compliance. A single violation—investment advice without registration, medical diagnosis without licensure, legal advice without bar admission—triggers regulatory enforcement with severe consequences. Current approaches rely on probabilistic compliance through model training or post-hoc filtering. These cannot provide the deterministic guarantees required by regulatory frameworks. We argue that architectural enforcement, not model training, is the necessary foundation for regulated AI deployment. By reconceptualizing execution itself as the enforcement mechanism, systems can provide guarantees that violations cannot reach users regardless of model behavior. This paper examines why probabilistic approaches are structurally insufficient, what properties an architectural solution must satisfy, and why this represents a fundamental shift in how AI safety is conceptualized. The core insight: execution completion should be conditional on validity, not optimistic. Systems that fail to generate compliant output should fail to generate any output. This fail-closed principle—borrowed from safety-critical engineering—provides the missing foundation for trustworthy AI in high-stakes domains.
