TI  - Tech Note 01: Financial Technology (Fintech) and Digital Remittances
AB  - Fintech and Digital RemittancesFintech (Financial Technology) refers to the use of digital and mobile technologies 1 to deliver speedy, lower-cost, 2 efficient, secure, convenient, accessible, and personalized financial services (payments, banking, lending, insurance, and investment).3 Fintech is also a broad term referring to digital financial technology utilized across a range of innovations, business models, sectors, and industries.Digital remittances 4 are online or mobile-enabled cross-border transfers from migrants to recipients through banks and non-bank providers (fintech apps, mobile money, e-payments, post offices, and online banking platforms).Innovations such as mobile money, digital payments, and blockchain (including cryptocurrencies and stablecoins), as well as artificial intelligence (AI), enable speedy, instant, 24/7 transfers and automation (chatbots and robo-advisors).Fintech and digital remittances expand digital and financial inclusion and support livelihoods (reducing poverty and improving access to food, healthcare, education, shelter, and clothing).Globally, fintech payments are expanding, driven by real-time systems, mobile wallets, open banking, AI, blockchain, and central bank digital currencies (CBDCs).Africa has emerged as a global leader in mobile money.Platforms 5 in the region offer cash and in-kind transfers (including food), e-commerce or eprocurement, bill payments or e-payments, airtime, loans, insurance, and card services through secure, affordable, and user-friendly mobile apps, as well as social media interfaces such as WhatsApp. Fintech Relevance for Migration and MigrantsFintech and digital remittance services play an essential role in advancing financial and digital inclusion for populations excluded from formal banking systems, including undocumented migrants, women, the unbanked and underbanked, low-income earners, informal traders and workers
