TI  - Auditing Memory Attribution in a Trading-Agent Replay
AB  - This paper presents a paired attribution audit of teacher-forced trading-agent replays using a frozen SPY historical replay. The study examines whether observed action changes can be attributed to retrieved return content rather than to surrounding risk wording, recommendations, or authority framing. Across three experiments, surrounding guidance reproduced the tested Llama action shifts, while the tested negative-only versus balanced return-composition substitutions produced no observed action switches. These results are configuration-specific and do not establish that retrieved facts were ignored. The task lacked an objective evidence-dependent decision rule, and the historical records do not provide a complete raw-attempt stream. The paper therefore treats the findings as a bounded attribution audit and identifies the evidence-use positive control required for stronger conclusions. No live trading, investment recommendation, or general claim about language-model memory is made.
