TI  - AI driven fintech adoption and financial decision making among Indian consumers: examining the roles of trust, perceived usefulness and perceived risk.
AB  - In the financial technology (FinTech) landscape, the swift development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has revolutionized the provision of financial services, from tailored financial solutions for personalization to automated investment advice, AI-driven chatbots, fraud prevention, and intelligent credit evaluation. Although AI-enabled FinTech services have been rapidly making headlines in India, there has been little research on the impact of these technologies on consumers' financial decision making after they've adopted these technologies. This research aims to present a conceptual framework to explore the relationship of perceived usefulness with trust, perceived risk and AI adoption of FinTech and its further impact on the financial decision making of Indian consumers. The study builds on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) to capture the behavioural and technological aspects of the financial service adoption of AI. Additionally, digital financial literacy and prior exposure to AI-based financial services are captured as moderators to shed light on consumer behavior in various segments. The design for the study is quantitative with a cross-sectional survey approach focusing on Indian consumers with experience of AI service in financial services. The conceptual model is used in an empirical validation method and is based on Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM) to test the direct, mediating and moderating relationships between the constructs. The proposed research will likely be a valuable addition to the existing body of knowledge on the adoption of financial information technology, which has recently expanded to incorporate AI technologies for financial decision-making. The results are expected to offer meaningful insights for FinTech firms, financial institutions, policy makers and technology developers in designing an AI-powered financial solution that is trustworthy, user-centric and secured, fostering informed financial decision making and building consumers' trust in India's fast-changing digital financial landscape.
