TI  - The Digital DOA: a guide for Australian boards on AI agents that can spend money
AB  - AI agents are starting to book freight, pay suppliers and accept quotes without a person approving each step. Most companies have rules for the people who can spend their money, and almost none for the software that now can. This paper argues that the answer is a tool finance teams already run: the delegation of authority, the table that says who can approve what, up to what amount. It sets out a version for AI agents, which I call the Digital DOA. It has three parts: a signature from the executive who answers for the agent, a set of spending limits, and a deal that gives that executive control of the limits and an off switch in return for carrying the risk. A worked example follows a retail freight agent through a $130,000 decision, and shows the limits holding when the data feeding the agent is wrong. The paper is written for Australian boards and finance leaders. It links the proposal to directors' duties under the Corporations Act, APRA's operational risk standard CPS 230, the Privacy Act changes starting 10 December 2026, and guidance from the Australian Institute of Company Directors. It draws on public cases including Zillow, Knight Capital, Air Canada's chatbot, Robodebt and the Medicare statistics portal incident disclosed in September 2026. It ends with a one-page register a board can use to list its agents that can spend and who has signed for each. The Digital DOA is a proposal, not a tested standard, and the figures are illustrative. This paper is general information, not legal or financial advice. Version 1.0, October 2026.
