TI  - Femuna: designing an integrated digital ecosystem for women across life domains
AB  - Background Despite the proliferation of mobile health (mHealth) applications, women continue to manage reproductive health, mental wellness, financial planning, and caregiving responsibilities across disconnected, single-purpose tools—a fragmentation that increases cognitive load, reduces long-term engagement, and remains underexplored in the mHealth literature. Objectives This work presents Femuna, an integrated cross-platform application designed around the principle of functional interdependence, and evaluates whether coherence-centered design measurably improves the user experience of women managing multiple life domains. Methods Femuna was implemented in Flutter and Firebase, combining task management, menstrual and pregnancy tracking, emotion monitoring, financial planning, educational content, and peer community support over a shared data layer through which features inform one another and drive adaptive notifications, and an AI mental-wellness chatbot deployed via a prompt-engineered Mistral 7B (BERTScore F1 = 0.8406). Usability was assessed through a mixed-methods design combining a concurrent think-aloud protocol with a five-point Likert-scale questionnaire, administered to a purposive sample of 185 women (ages 18–50) across diverse occupational and family contexts. Results Questionnaire responses yielded an aggregate mean usability score of 4.2 on the five-point scale, indicating consistently high satisfaction across all evaluated dimensions, and thematic analysis of session transcripts showed that participants particularly valued functional integration, reporting reduced task-switching burden and an interface organization aligned with the relational structure of their daily responsibilities. Conclusions The findings support coherence-centered design as a measurable quality criterion in mHealth development and ground a replicable framework for gender-responsive applications based on feminist HCI principles, integrated systems architecture, and participatory usability evaluation.
