TI  - Impact of Artificial Intelligence tools on learning outcomes of commerce students
AB  - Can digital intelligence reshape how commerce graduates acquire and apply knowledge? This question drives the present research, which examined the effect of artificial intelligence (AI) tools on learning outcomes among undergraduate commerce students in India. A quasi-experimental, non-equivalent control group design was adopted, involving 240 second-year Bachelor of Commerce students drawn from two government-affiliated colleges in New Delhi. Participants were divided into a control group (n = 120) that received conventional lecture-based instruction and an experimental group (n = 120) that used AI-assisted platforms including ChatGPT for conceptual queries and practice problem generation, Grammarly for academic writing feedback, Quillbot for paraphrase-based learning exercises, and Canva AI for visual presentation tasks over a 12-week semester. Pre-test and post-test scores across six learning domains (accounting knowledge, business analytics, financial literacy, research skills, digital competency, and overall performance) served as the main outcome measures, while a structured 31-item perception survey captured student attitudes toward AI adoption. Results showed that students in the experimental group recorded markedly higher post-test scores compared with the control group across all six domains (p < 0.01), with effect sizes (Cohen's d) ranging from 1.67 to 3.09. The largest improvements appeared in digital competency (mean gain 27.2 percentage points) and financial literacy (mean gain 17.4 percentage points). Survey data pointed to a favourable disposition toward AI tools, with 82.3% of respondents describing them as beneficial for self-directed learning. Regression analysis confirmed that frequency of AI tool use, prior digital exposure, and student motivation together explained 61.7% of the variance in post-test performance, with usage frequency emerging as the strongest predictor. Of particular interest, students with the least prior digital experience showed the largest gains, suggesting that structured AI integration can act as an equaliser rather than widening existing skill gaps. These findings suggest that structured integration of AI platforms into commerce curricula can meaningfully improve both cognitive and skill-based learning outcomes, and they offer practical guidance for educators seeking to modernise pedagogy in Indian higher education.
