TI  - Artificial Intelligence and FinTech Innovation in Emerging Markets: The Role of Digital Financial Capabilities and Customer Trust
AB  - This paper investigates the role of Artificial Intelligence (AI), Digital Financial Capabilities (DFC) and Customer Trust in digital FinTech Innovation in emerging markets. The statistical research design used was quantitative, cross-sectional, and a questionnaire with five-point Likert scales was used for the collection of data, reaching 300 adult FinTech users. Intelligent automation, predictive analytics, personalized financial services, fraud detection and AI powered decision-support were used to evaluate the impact of AI. The impact of AI was evaluated using the following trends: intelligent automation, predictive analytics, personalized financial services, fraud detection, and AI powered decision-support. DFC identified the skills that the users required to access, understand, assess and safely utilize digital financial technologies and Customer Trust addressed security, reliability, transparency, privacy and responsible service practices. The concept of FinTech Innovation stood for better digital financial products and service delivery, customer experience, personalization and efficiency. Descriptive findings indicated positive perceptions on all constructs ranging from mean scores of 3.92 (SD = 0.71) for AI to 3.79 (SD = 0.76) for Customer Trust and 3.95 (SD = 0.69) for FinTech Innovation for DFC. The reliability tests showed low item-total correlation, with the overall .24 item-total alpha range from .89 (AI) to .92 (FinTech Innovation), and .91 (Customer Trust), .87 (DFC), and .89 (AI). The results highlighted that the capital markets in emerging markets need technological intelligence, users who can apply the technology and trustworthy digital environments. It is crucial to address issues of customer-friendly technology governance, customer security, privacy safeguarding, transparency, and transformational digital financial education in the governance of inclusive and sustainable innovations in the financial sector with the help of artificial intelligence technologies, the study has pointed out. References Abbas, N., & Khan, H. G. M. (2024). The role of financial literacy and technology savviness on FinTech adoption in traditional banking, the mediating role of trust in technology. Pakistan Journal of Humanities and Social Sciences, 12(4), 3183–3191. https://doi.org/10.52131/pjhss.2024.v12i4.2577 Aftab, R., Fazal, A., & Andleeb, R. (2025). Behavioral biases and FinTech adoption: Investigating the role of financial literacy. Acta Psychologica, 257, Article 105065. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.actpsy.2025.105065 Akanfe, O., Bhatt, P., & Lawong, D. A. (2025). Technology advancements shaping the financial inclusion landscape: Present interventions, emergence of artificial intelligence and future directions. Information Systems Frontiers, 27, 2189–2212. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10796-025-10597-z Akbar, U., Asghar, F., & Arshad, M. (2025). FinTech disruption and transformation in banking: Mediating effect of artificial intelligence. Advance Journal of Econometrics and Finance, 3(2), 210–223. https://doi.org/10.63075/99vmf939 Akhtar, M., Salman, A., Ghafoor, K. A., & Kamran, M. (2024). Artificial intelligence, financial services knowledge, government support, and user innovativeness: Exploring the moderated-mediated path to fintech adoption. Heliyon, 10(21), Article e39521. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e39521 Al Khub, A., Saeudy, M., & Gerged, A. M. (2024). Digital financial inclusion in emerging economies: Evidence from Jordan. Journal of Risk and Financial Management, 17(2), Article 66. https://doi.org/10.3390/jrfm17020066 Amin, F. (2025, November). Sentinel Polity: LLM-grounded policy-as-code for IaC hardening in hyperscale clouds. In International Conference on Data Science, Computation and Security (pp. 326–335). Springer Nature Switzerland. Amin, F. (2026). Adaptive cyber risk management. In Data science and security: Proceedings of IDSCS 2025 (Vol. 2, p. 222). Springer. Appiah, T., & Agblewornu, V. V. (