TI  - Islamic Banking and the Sabbath Pond Test: A Re-Evaluation through Classical Islamic Economic Sources
AB  - This paper re-examines the practices of modern Islamic banking through the lens of classical Islamic economic principles. By applying what we term the “Sabbath Pond Test”—a metaphor drawn from the Qur’anic account of a community that circumvented the prohibition on fishing on the Sabbath—we assess whether contemporary Islamic financial products avoid the substance of riba (usury) or merely its form. Drawing on Qur’anic injunctions, Prophetic Hadith, and Hanafi jurisprudence (including works like Ahkam al-Qur’an, Kitab al-Amwal, Kitab al-Kharaj, and Usul al-Shashi), the paper critically analyzes a hypothetical case study of an Islamic mortgage structure that imitates an interest-bearing loan via sale, lease, and buy-back arrangements. We then present classical, interest-free financing alternatives such as Musharakah and Mudarabah, as well as public finance measures from early Islamic history that prioritized risk-sharing and social welfare. The study finds that while Islamic banks like Meezan Bank claim Sharī‘ah compliance by contract technicalities, there is an urgent need for greater transparency and alignment with the maqasid (objectives) of Sharī‘ah — emphasizing justice and economic substance over form. AI Assistance Disclosure: Claude (Anthropic) and ChatGPT (OpenAI) were used for language refinement, paraphrasing, structural editing, clarity improvements, formatting and citation-style consistency checks, preliminary source discovery and citation suggestions, and translation into other languages. The idea of applying the Sabbath Pond analogy to contemporary Islamic banking originated with the author. The author is accountable for the manuscript’s content; no AI tool is credited as an author. Academic preprint; not a fatwa or financial advice. Archived at Zenodo: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21545850. Web edition: https://irshados.com/ebooks/islamic-banking-sabbath-pond-test-reevaluation/
